Inode tables zero in runs, not blocks

An inode table is physically contiguous and its initial content is zeroes
-- the textbook bulk write -- and it went through the block cache one
4 KiB command at a time: ~512 commands per group, ~66,000 for a 16 GB
format. That is the "minutes of silence" the format progress bar exists to
cover, priced entirely in per-command cost on real media (the byte volume
is a modest ~260 MB).

The same loop runs at MOUNT. A volume formatted by mke2fs with
lazy_itable_init (a common default) leaves groups INODE_UNINIT; the
post-recovery walk in __ext4_recover touches every group and initializes
each uninitialized table -- with a journal running, every block ALSO
traveled through the log. On a dirty lazy-formatted stick that work lands
inside DiskArbitration's ~20 s budget, the replay incident's shape from a
different door.

The table now zeroes in physically-contiguous runs, one device command
per 1 MiB chunk, past the cache. Crash safety is by idempotence and
ordering: the zeroes carry no state, and the UNINIT flag that declares
the table valid is journaled by the caller and commits behind a barrier
AFTER these writes -- a crash before the commit re-zeroes next time, a
crash after finds the zeroes on the medium. Cached copies are invalidated
so nothing resurrects stale bytes; the old per-block path remains as the
out-of-memory fallback.

Measured: a 2 GB format falls from ~8,300 write commands to ~105; a dirty
lazy-formatted volume's recovery mount from ~7,000 to ~77. Red cells with
those bounds (and e2fsck over the results) live in
Tests/run_format_tests.sh.

